This is easy to understand but doesn't actually manipulate the original nodes.

Approach 2 — Stack
Idea

Process the linked list group by group.

For every k nodes:

Push them into a stack.
Pop them to get reversed order.
Connect them to the result.

If fewer than k nodes remain, leave them as they are.

Code

class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseKGroup(ListNode head, int k) {

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode tail = dummy;

        ListNode curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {

            Stack<ListNode> stack = new Stack<>();

            ListNode temp = curr;
            int count = 0;

            // Collect k nodes
            while (temp != null && count < k) {
                stack.push(temp);
                temp = temp.next;
                count++;
            }

            // Fewer than k nodes
            if (count < k) {
                tail.next = curr;
                break;
            }

            // Pop in reverse order
            while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                ListNode node = stack.pop();

                tail.next = node;
                tail = node;
            }

            curr = temp;
        }

        tail.next = null;

        return dummy.next;
    }
}

